{# The support ask, as an overlay over the page you are already on.

   This used to be a link to getmarquee.now/#support in a new tab. The ask is one
   mark, one heading, one paragraph and one button — small enough to answer where
   it is made, and small enough that a page load spent on it is one the user did
   not ask for. The Buy Me a Coffee button is the only thing here that still
   leaves the app, because the payment page genuinely is elsewhere.

   A `.modal`, not a `.modal` and a `.sheet`. "Centred dialog on a pointer screen,
   app-style tray on a phone" is not two components in this stylesheet — it is
   what `.modal` already is: the `@media (max-width: 640px)` block docks the panel
   to the bottom edge, restores the grab handle, hides the × and re-points the
   entrance, and initSheetGestures in gallery.js already claims `.modal__head` as
   a drag target. Rendering a second copy for narrow screens would duplicate the
   copy and hand the focus manager two dialogs to choose between.

   Teleported to <body>, for the reason written up at length in _menu.html.twig
   and just as load-bearing here. This partial is included inside
   `<header class="topbar">`, because that is where the `supportOpen` scope lives;
   the header carries `backdrop-filter`, and that makes an element a containing
   block for its fixed-position descendants. Without the teleport this overlay's
   `position: fixed; inset: 0` resolves against the header's own box and the whole
   thing renders squashed into the height of the bar that opened it. It looks
   correct on a desktop viewport either way, which is exactly why this is written
   down rather than left to be rediscovered.

   Not in _overlays.html.twig, which is the other place a shared overlay could
   plausibly live. That file binds against the overlay component mixed into a
   page's own Alpine root (galleryUI, orphansPage) and is included only by pages
   that have one. This overlay binds against the layout's nav scope and has to
   render on every page that draws navigation — including the ones that include no
   _overlays.html.twig at all. #}
{% import 'partials/_icons.html.twig' as icons %}
{% import 'partials/_transitions.html.twig' as tx %}

<template x-teleport="body">
<div class="modal" x-show="supportOpen" x-cloak {{ tx.dialog() }}
     @keydown.escape.window="supportOpen = false" style="display:none">
    {# Closing on click is what makes the phone gesture work as well as the tap:
       a completed drag-to-dismiss synthesises a click on this element rather than
       reaching for a component's method — see initSheetGestures in gallery.js. #}
    <div class="modal__backdrop" @click="supportOpen = false"></div>
    {# The default width, not --narrow. That variant is 380px and exists for a
       confirmation, which is one line of question and two buttons; this is a
       paragraph, and at 380px it sets to six lines with a ragged right. The 440px
       default takes it to five and reads as a card rather than a column. #}
    <div class="modal__panel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
         aria-label="Support development" tabindex="-1">
        {# Hidden on desktop, the drag target on a phone. Must be a real element
           and a different one from the scrolling body; see the note in
           _overlays.html.twig. #}
        <div class="sheet__grip"><span class="sheet__handle"></span></div>
        {# The mark sits inside the heading rather than above the copy, and that is
           the whole composition. On its own line beneath the head it belonged to
           neither the heading it named nor the copy it introduced, and read as
           floating. Bound to the heading it is one piece, so the two centre
           together and the mark can never drift from its words. #}
        <div class="modal__head support-ask__head">
            <h2 class="support-ask__title">
                {# The same heart the Support Development entry wears. Drawn from the
                   shared icon set rather than ported from the site, so it is one
                   glyph in one drawing style. #}
                <span class="support-ask__mark" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons.icon('support', 20) }}</span>
                Support development
            </h2>
            <button type="button" class="modal__close" @click="supportOpen = false"
                    aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
        </div>
        <div class="modal__body support-ask">
            <p class="support-ask__text">
                Marquee is free and always will be. If it earned a spot in your setup, you can
                throw a few dollars at ongoing development &mdash; or, let's be honest, at another
                hard drive for my Plex server that I absolutely need and can definitely justify.
            </p>
            {# Dismisses on the way out, like every other new-tab destination in the
               application — the menu tray closes itself when Poster Wall is chosen
               for the same reason. The ask has been answered; leaving it standing
               over the page means the user comes back from the payment tab to a
               dialog still asking. #}
            <a class="btn btn--accent support-ask__cta" @click="supportOpen = false"
               href="https://www.buymeacoffee.com/jeremehancock" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                Hard drive fund
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
</template>
